is on canadian soil  a good place to start they say a new  national security review the last one  was done in 2004. chris  david aiken in ottawa thank you david  the pen is mightier than the  flamethrower ahead why one of canada's  greatest novels is now fireproof  [Music]  canadian literary legend margaret atwood  has armed herself with a flamethrower to  protest censorship  the 82 year old is promoting the one-off  auction of a fully fire-resistant copy  of her novel the handmaid's tale atwood  and publisher penguin house say the  version was made in response to what  they call the challenging the banning  and even burning of hundreds of books  across the u.s  the prime minister is in bc visiting the  tecumless to schwetmick first nation as  it marks one year since it confirmed 215  unmarked presumed graves at a former  residential school trudeau met elders  and leaders in the community but not  everyone was happy to see him nitu  garcia explains  [Applause]  for many the prime minister's arrival  here monday was a surprise  he arrived to a day-long memorial event  marking one year since more than 200  unmarked burials were found near the  former kamloops indian residential  school around 4 30 pm to a mixed  reaction there were many supporters but  others didn't want him present this is  about our elders and our survivors those  who are still filled with hurt and anger  i hear that as well prime minister  trudeau fell short of making any  concrete commitments a sign of his  honest intent to focus on survivors say  some but to leaders like grand chief  stuart phillip who wants funding for  more ground-penetrating radar searches  it was a performative approach there  needs to be  a significant investment in this work  and  you know we expect the prime minister to  acknowledge that and understand that and  i heard some of the survivors saying  that like what did he really say  was this just a photo op  trudeau vowed to partner with the  community in its next steps and he  addressed the anger  what so many people have been through  in terms of layers of trauma that they  have gone through if if it makes anyone  feel a little bit better to shout at me  than please they should cook p roseanne  kasmir focusing on the importance of  moving beyond blame and working together  something amanda big sorrel horse agrees  with  i appreciate what he did say and  hopefully that he will have support to  move forward in a more positive and  impactful way for all of us not just us  as indigenous and native people but for  all canadians this is canadian history  last year prime minister trudeau faced  criticism for vacationing on vancouver  island instead of coming here for the  country's first national day for truth  and reconciliation and while he didn't  provide specifics on previous promises  like a local healing center for tacoma  loops to schwetmick he did say the  federal government will support whatever  path forward communities like this one  decide is right for them chris  all right thanks very much nithu garcia  reporting in kamloops and a reminder  there is support available for survivors  of residential schools and their  families the number is toll free  operates 24 hours a day it is  1-800-721-0066  soul food coming up next how traumatized  ukrainians are finding peace and  friendship in the kitchen  [Music]  for those who remain in ukraine life is  seemingly under constant threat but  through that trauma the kitchen is a  place where people are finding food  and friendship crystal gomencing reports  in this kitchen in kiev food is love and  with every dish comfort is served if you  worry about someone you want at first  you want to feed them  that's care that's how we care about our  loved ones everyone around this table  agrees the war has made them family and  they've been cooking for about ten  thousand extended relatives  soldiers people in hospital neighborhood  kids  anyone in need it's better than just sit  at home and scroll news and  do nothing  nothing good comes from passing time  doom scrolling on social media so in the  days following february 24th more than  two dozen cafe restaurant owners bakers  and workers decided to put their skills  to use and get to work they united to  find kitchen space food donations  and delivery drivers the main cool thing  that we did we connected people who were  panicked shocked and they were in fear  and we make their life full of sense  because they have a job they  can help and they can see results very  quickly most of the team is under 35  many left the capitol when the city was  under attack in the early days of the  full invasion  being away however made them feel  disconnected  and vulnerable when you're doing  something good when you are helping  maybe you are not realizing that first  of all you are helping yourself and then  it's like in the airplane you have to  put on the the mask on yourself first  and then for the other people  as ukraine secures more of its territory  demand is beginning to drop but key  volunteers haven't stopped they're still  cooking but also collecting and  delivering humanitarian supplies too  anything to support each other  and their extended family  crystal gamson global news  london  and that is global national for this  tuesday night i'm chris galas thanks for  watching tonight's your canada is  halifax's waterfront i'll see you back  here tomorrow hope you have a great  evening  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]
 

